{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high vs realized prices, especially beyond ~day 8–10. Apply a downward level shift (larger for longer horizons).",
    "Downtrend underestimation: when recent history shows a clear decline, the model assumes too much rebound/mean reversion and misses continued weakness. Reduce mean-reversion strength and increase trend-following in falling regimes.",
    "Hump-shaped overshoot: forecasts often rise into days ~5–15 (local peak) while outcomes keep drifting lower. Damp mid-horizon upward curvature; enforce monotonicity/low-slope constraints when momentum is negative.",
    "Volatility/turning-point mis-timing (lag): model reacts late to regime shifts; it projects prior range/trend for too long, then corrects too slowly. Increase weight on the most recent 3–7 days and allow faster level resets.",
    "Horizon-dependent error growth: errors widen with horizon (short-term close, long-term too high). Use horizon-scaled bias correction (e.g., small adjustment days 1–3, moderate days 4–10, largest days 11–30).",
    "Flooring effect: model rarely forecasts the deep drawdowns seen in outcomes (low 60s) and keeps prices in a higher band. Add tail-risk/downside allowance when recent lows are being broken."
  ]
}
